%S A400859 1,27,324,800,2025,3969,4800,9801,11200,12500,13689,17600,20800,21600,
%T A400859 23409,27200,29241,30400,36000,36800,42849,46400,48600,49600,59200,
%U A400859 65600,67200,68121,68800,75200,77841,84800,86400,94400,97600,105600,107200,110889,113600,116800
%N A400859 Numbers of the form k * d(k), where k is a refactorable number (A033950) satisfying d(k/d(k)) = d(d(k)), where d(x) is the divisor count of x.
%C A400859 This sequence is formed by multiplying a divisor-symmetric subset [satisfying d(k/d(k)) = d(d(k)] of the refactorable (or tau) numbers (A033950) by their own divisor counts.
%C A400859 The purpose of this sequence is to isolate the strict intersection between refactorable (tau) numbers and recursive divisor equivalences within the A036438 family.
%C A400859 This sequence is infinite as it contains 1600 * p where gcd(p, 1600) = 1. Proof: 1600 * p = (80*p) * 20 = (80*p) * d(80*p) and 20 | 80*p. - _David A. Corneth_, Oct 09 2026
%C A400859 This sequence has at least 12 finite solution sets for valid d(k) where W = (k * d(k))
%C A400859   d(k)=1 W = 1 * 1 = 1
%C A400859   d(k)=3 W = 9 * 3 = 27
%C A400859   d(k)=10 W = 80 * 10 = 800; and W = 1250 * 10 = 12500
%C A400859   d(k)=30 W = 720 * 30 = 21600; W = 1200 * 30 = 36000; W = 1620 * 30 = 48600; W = 4050 * 30 = 121500; W = 7500 * 30 = 225000; and W = 11250 * 30 = 337500
%C A400859   d(k)=60 (finite) W = 40500 * 60 = 2430000; and W = 67500 * 60 = 4050000
%C A400859 Some identified infinite solution sets include:
%C A400859   d(k)=9: W = 81 * p^2 (excl 3)
%C A400859   d(k)=20: W = 1600 * p (excl 2, 5)
%C A400859   d(k)=40 (3 templates): W = 3200 * p^3, 200000 * p, 12800 * p1^4 * p2 (excl 2, 5)
%C A400859   d(k)=60 (2 templates): W = 43200 * p, 72000 * p (excl 2, 3, 5)
%C A400859   d(k)=72 (3 templates): W = 20736 * p^3, 139968 * p^2, 31104 * p1^2 * p2 (excl 2, 3)
%C A400859   d(k)=84 (2 templates): W = 1714608 * p, 118590192 * p (excl 2, 3, 7)
%C A400859   d(k)=90 (2 templates): W = 64800 * p^2, 1012500 * p^2 (excl 2, 3, 5)
%C A400859   d(k)=96 (2 templates): W = 82944 * p^3, 248832 * p (excl 2, 3)
%C A400859   d(k)=120 (6 templates): W = [86400, 144000] * p^3, [345600, 576000] * p, [115200, 192000] * p1^4 * p2 (excl 2, 3, 5)
%C A400859   d(k)=180 (4 templates): W = [129600, 291600] * p^5 or * p1^2 * p2 (excl 2, 3, 5)
%C A400859   d(k)=240 (2 templates): W = 115200 * p^9, 115200 * p1^4 * p2 (excl 2, 3, 5)
%C A400859   d(k)=360 (6 templates): W = 259200 * [p^11, p1^5*p2, p1^3*p2^2, p1^2*p2*p3] and 16200000 * [p^5, p1^2*p2] (excl 2, 3, 5)
%D A400859 Tom M. Apostol, Introduction to Analytic Number Theory, Springer-Verlag, 1976, pp. 29 ff. James J. Tattersall, Elementary Number Theory in Nine Chapters, Cambridge University Press, 1999, page 162.
%D A400859 Richard K. Guy, Unsolved Problems in Number Theory, 3rd Edition, Springer, 2004, Section B12, pp. 102-103. New Scientist, Sep 05 1998, p. 17, para. 3.
%D A400859 S. Ramanujan, Collected Papers, Ed. G. H. Hardy et al., Cambridge 1927; Chelsea, NY, 1962.
%H A400859 David A. Corneth, <a href="/A400859/b400859.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%e A400859 21600 is in the sequence because it is generated by k = 720. The divisor count is d(720) = 30. 720 is a multiple of 30, satisfying the refactorable condition. The cofactor is 720 / 30 = 24. The divisor count of the cofactor is d(24) = 8, which exactly equals the divisor count of the original divisor count, d(30) = 8. The resulting value is 720 * 30 = 21600.
%o A400859 (PARI) isok(n) = fordiv (n, k, my(dk=numdiv(k)); if ((k*dk == n) && !(k%dk) && (numdiv(k/dk) == numdiv(dk)), return (1))); \\ _Michel Marcus_, Oct 09 2026
